<Base title="Types and extensions in PostgreSQL" description="Use ranges to prevent overlapping bookings, temporal keys to require continuous coverage, and PostGIS to combine geographic distance with ordinary relational rules.">
<article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">PostgreSQL</p><h1>Types and extensions in PostgreSQL</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>PostgreSQL 18</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/constraints/">Constraints ↗</a></div></header>
 <p>A database type determines which values a column can hold and which operations make sense on them. A booking interval has more structure than two unrelated timestamps: it includes some instants and excludes others, and it may overlap another interval. A geographic location similarly needs coordinate and distance rules before “within one kilometre” has a dependable meaning.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL lets types, operators and index support work together. Some combinations are built in. Others arrive as extensions: packages of database objects, sometimes backed by compiled libraries. The useful question is what behaviour the combination provides for an application, and which parts its operator must install and maintain.</p>
 <p>We will follow a booking service through two decisions: prevent overlapping room reservations and find a suitable nearby venue. A short temporal-constraint example adds the requirement that a worker's authorisation cover an entire assignment. The mechanisms are different, but each replaces a vague representation with a value and operations that express the actual question.</p>
 <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#ranges">A range as one value</a></li><li><a href="#exclusion">Exclusion constraints for overlapping bookings</a></li><li><a href="#coverage">Temporal foreign keys and period coverage</a></li><li><a href="#spatial">PostGIS geography and distance queries</a></li><li><a href="#extension">Installing and maintaining extensions</a></li></ol></nav>

 <section id="ranges"><h2>A range as one value</h2>
 <p>Room Cedar is booked from 10:00 until 11:00 on 15 October 2026. We include the starting instant and exclude the ending instant. That choice allows the next booking to begin exactly at 11:00 without sharing any time with the first.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL's built-in <code>tsrange</code> holds a range of timestamps. The notation <code>[2026-10-15 10:00,2026-10-15 11:00)</code> records both bounds and that endpoint policy: the square bracket includes 10:00; the closing parenthesis excludes 11:00. The range is one typed value, so its overlap operator can apply a consistent rule to every pair.</p>
 <p>These venue examples use one local civil-time schedule. A service coordinating absolute instants across time zones would normally make that requirement explicit with <code>tstzrange</code>, whose bounds use timestamps with time zone. Changing the range type does not remove the need to decide how local booking times are interpreted around clock changes.</p>
 <Ranges />
 <p>The range operator <code>&amp;&amp;</code> asks whether two values share any point. The adjacent 10:00–11:00 and 11:00–12:00 ranges do not. A 10:30–11:30 request overlaps both. Equality of the start and end pairs would miss that conflict because neither existing range is exactly equal to the requested one.</p>
 <p>Ranges can also be empty or unbounded. Those are meaningful values in some applications, but our room bookings must occupy a nonempty interval with finite bounds. The table therefore checks those properties in addition to requiring a nonnull range. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/rangetypes.html">range documentation</a> develops these value and endpoint rules.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="exclusion"><h2>Exclusion constraints for overlapping bookings</h2>
 <p>The service can search for available time before showing a room, but that read cannot reserve it. Two requests may both see the same gap before either writes. We need a rule that the database checks when accepting a new reservation.</p>
 <p>The following is the table definition used by the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-range-rules">booking and period lesson</a>. Run that lesson's setup once, then its insert steps; the setup already creates this table and the initial 10:00–11:00 Cedar booking.</p>
 <Sql code={booking} label="A typed interval with a same-room overlap constraint" />
 <p>An exclusion constraint rejects a pair of rows when all the listed operator comparisons are true. Here that means <em>same room</em> and <em>overlapping time</em>. Two Cedar rows with disjoint times pass. Two rows with overlapping times in different rooms also pass. The rule does not prohibit every overlap in the building.</p>
 <p><code>USING gist</code> supplies an index method that can support these comparisons. GiST is a framework; the operator classes for each type supply its search behaviour. Range support is built in. The supplied <code>btree_gist</code> extension adds suitable support for scalar types such as our text room name, allowing its equality test to join the range overlap test in this index.</p>
 <Sql code={sql.adjacent} label="The adjacent Cedar booking succeeds" />
 <p>This returns booking 2. Now send the 10:30–11:30 request for Cedar:</p>
 <Sql code={sql.overlap} label="The overlapping Cedar request is rejected" />
 <p>PostgreSQL returns SQLSTATE <code>23P01</code>, an exclusion-constraint violation. No booking 3 was inserted. The handler can tell the caller that the requested time is unavailable and offer another choice. If it opened an explicit transaction, it must roll it back before continuing. It cannot treat the attempted insert as a reservation merely because its earlier availability query passed.</p>
 <p>The lesson then retries the same time in Birch. The insert succeeds because room equality is false, even though the time ranges overlap Cedar's bookings. This completes the choice: the database enforces the room boundary we intended.</p>
 <p>We also tested a real concurrent case on PostgreSQL 18.6. Connection A inserted Cedar 12:00–13:00 and kept its transaction open. Connection B attempted Cedar 12:30–13:30. B waited on A's transaction; after A committed, B received <code>23P01</code>. The index-backed constraint did not let both writes succeed just because B lacked an ordinary committed view of A's row.</p>
 <p>The browser lesson demonstrates the accepted and rejected values on one connection. The two-connection observation is separate server evidence. A successful insert inside an open transaction is still tentative until that transaction commits, just as with other reservation work.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="coverage"><h2>Temporal foreign keys and period coverage</h2>
 <p>Overlap is one temporal question. A staffing rule asks another: is a worker authorised for <em>every day</em> of an assignment? Finding one intersecting authorisation is insufficient if it leaves the last day uncovered.</p>
 <p>Worker 7 has two authorisation records: 1 October up to but excluding 16 October, and 16 October up to but excluding 1 November. Together they cover October continuously. An assignment from 10 October up to 20 October crosses their boundary; neither authorisation alone contains it.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL 18 adds temporal keys with <code>WITHOUT OVERLAPS</code> and foreign keys marked <code>PERIOD</code>. Our setup defines:</p>
 <Sql code={temporal} label="A non-overlapping temporal key and full-period reference" />
 <p>The authorisation key allows repeated worker IDs while preventing overlapping valid ranges for the same worker. It uses GiST rather than an ordinary equality-only unique B-tree; the scalar worker ID again uses btree_gist support. The last column must be a range or multirange, and an empty temporal-key range is not allowed.</p>
 <p>The assignment's foreign key compares worker ID for equality and requires the referenced periods, combined, to cover the assignment's entire range. Our 10–20 October assignment succeeds using both records. Deleting the second authorisation while that assignment remains would destroy coverage, so PostgreSQL rejects the parent change with foreign-key error <code>23503</code>.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Continuous coverage and a one-day gap"><table><thead><tr><th>Worker 7's authorisations</th><th>Assignment [10 Oct, 20 Oct)</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>[1 Oct, 16 Oct) + [16 Oct, 1 Nov)</td><td>Accepted</td><td>Coverage meets at 16 Oct</td></tr><tr><td>[1 Oct, 16 Oct) + [17 Oct, 1 Nov)</td><td>Rejected</td><td>16 Oct is uncovered</td></tr><tr><td>Restore the second start to 16 Oct</td><td>Accepted again</td><td>The whole period is covered</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>To create the second case in the lesson, we first remove the practice assignment, then move the second authorisation's start to 17 October in a completed editor run. Retrying the assignment in the next run fails. Both authorisations overlap part of it, but the day-long gap breaks the stronger coverage rule. Closing the gap permits the assignment again.</p>
 <p>These constraints express valid-time relationships. They do not automatically retain the old authorisation rows when we edit them or supply an audit history. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createtable.html">PostgreSQL 18 CREATE TABLE reference</a> states their exact key and reference requirements. Historical recording remains a separate data-design decision.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="spatial"><h2>PostGIS geography and distance queries</h2>
 <p>The same booking service now wants a venue within one kilometre that has a room for at least eight people. A longitude and latitude can identify a point, but subtracting them does not give metres. Longitude degrees also cover different ground distances at different latitudes.</p>
 <p><strong>PostGIS</strong> is an external extension that supplies spatial types and operations. For this query we use <code>geography(Point,4326)</code>: points in a geographic coordinate system, with distance operations measured over the Earth's surface. The geometry type instead measures in the units of its spatial reference system. Both can be useful; the units are part of the design.</p>
 <p>Our query point is longitude 18.0686, latitude 59.3293. Four invented venues use the coordinates below. The distances were calculated on the checked PostGIS 3.6.4 server with its default spheroidal geography distance.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Venue locations, measured distances and eligibility"><table><thead><tr><th>Venue / coordinates</th><th>Distance</th><th>Rooms</th><th>Open for booking?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Centre · 18.0686, 59.3293</td><td>0 m</td><td>4 seats</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>North · 18.0686, 59.3343</td><td>557.0 m</td><td>12 and 6 seats</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>East · 18.0736, 59.3293</td><td>284.6 m</td><td>10 seats</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Far north · 18.0686, 59.3493</td><td>2,228.0 m</td><td>20 seats</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>The nearest venue is too small. East has enough seats and is nearby but is closed. Only North satisfies all three conditions within one kilometre. Location belongs to the venue row, while the room capacities remain related rows:</p>
 <Sql code={spatial} label="Combine geographic distance with relational eligibility" />
 <p><code>ST_MakePoint</code> receives longitude first, then latitude. <code>ST_SetSRID</code> declares the coordinate system of these already-correct values; it does not transform coordinates from another system. Casting to geography gives <code>ST_DWithin</code> a threshold measured in metres. The <code>EXISTS</code> subquery asks whether a suitable room exists without repeating the venue once per matching room.</p>
 <p><a href={spatialExperiment} download="spatial-experiment.sql">Download the executed spatial experiment</a> for a disposable PostgreSQL database with PostGIS installed. It creates the venues and rooms, reports distances, and changes the radius from 1,000 to 3,000 metres. That variation returns North and Far north. It does not make Centre bigger or reopen East.</p>
 <p>With many venues, a spatial GiST index can narrow the distance test. A spatial operator class stores bounds suitable for excluding regions. A bounding region can include points outside the requested distance, so the exact predicate still checks candidates. <a href="https://postgis.net/docs/manual-3.6/ST_DWithin.html">ST_DWithin</a> includes an index-aware bounding comparison.</p>
 <p>The download also creates a regular grid of 10,000 geographic points and a GiST index. In our run, the index supplied 189 candidates for the 1,000-metre question. The distance filter removed 66, leaving 123. Those counts show the two stages: cheaply narrow the region, then determine which candidates satisfy the actual distance. Treating all index candidates as exact matches would give the wrong answer.</p>
 <p>Geographic proximity is still only one eligibility condition. This query discovers a venue with a large enough room; it does not reserve a time. When the caller chooses a room and interval, the booking constraint remains the authority for accepting the reservation.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="extension"><h2>Installing and maintaining extensions</h2>
 <p>Three different installation boundaries appeared here. Range types, their overlap operator and temporal constraints are built into PostgreSQL 18. <code>btree_gist</code> ships with PostgreSQL's supplied modules but must be available in the server installation and enabled in the database. PostGIS and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/vector-search/">pgvector</a> are separately versioned external projects.</p>
 <p><code>CREATE EXTENSION</code> loads an available extension's database objects; it does not download and compile its package. A managed service may expose a supported set and version. On a self-managed server, the matching library and extension files need to be installed first. Inspect both availability and what this particular database has enabled:</p>
 <Sql code={versions} label="Server availability and database-installed versions are separate facts" />
 <p>Privileges also belong to that boundary. PostgreSQL permits an extension marked trusted to be installed by a role with the required database privilege; other extensions can require stronger privileges. Successful installation as an administrator does not mean the ordinary application role should own or modify extension objects. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createextension.html">CREATE EXTENSION reference</a> describes this distinction.</p>
 <p>The extension's objects then become dependencies of ordinary schema objects. Our geography column needs its type, and the spatial index needs its operator support. A restore destination must have compatible extension software before restoring those dependent objects. A library upgrade and a database-level extension update are related but distinct operations; verify the installed version and the extension's upgrade procedure rather than assuming that replacing a package changed every database.</p>
 <p>The reward is that the spatial or temporal value participates in the same queries and transactions as the rest of the model. A room interval can be constrained at write time; a geographic predicate can combine with room capacity; neither requires abandoning relational structure. Whether that is the right home for a larger workload depends on the supported behaviour, resource use and recovery procedures the team can actually maintain.</p>
 </section>
